</form><html xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:math="java.lang.Math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:fo="http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Format" xmlns:fn="http://www.w3.org/2005/02/xpath-functions" xmlns:xdt="http://www.w3.org/2005/02/xpath-datatypes" xmlns:caml="http://lc.ca.gov/legalservices/schemas/caml.1#" xmlns:xhtml="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><head><title>HR131:v99#DOCUMENT</title><meta name="dc:identifier" content="HR131"/><meta name="dc:title" content="HR &#xA;              131-&#xA;                INTRODUCED&#xA;              "/><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html;charset=utf-8"/></head><body><span class="hidden"><h2>Bill Start</h2></span><div id="bill_all" align="justify"><div id="about"><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><table style="border-collapse: collapse" cellSpacing="0" align="center" role="presentation"><tbody><tr><td align="center"/></tr></tbody></table></div><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><font size="3" style="FONT-FAMILY: MinionPC, Arial Unicode MS">
                    CALIFORNIA LEGISLATURE—
                    2025–2026 REGULAR SESSION</font></div><br clear="all"/><span id="bill_house_num"><div style="float: left; width:50%; font-weight: bold; "><span style="text-transform: uppercase; text-align:left;width:50%;"><b>
                House Resolution
              </b></span></div><div style="float:right; width:50%; text-align:right;"><span style="text-transform: uppercase;"><b>No. 131</b></span></div></span><hr/><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><table style="border-collapse: collapse" cellSpacing="0" align="center" role="presentation"><tbody><tr><td id="bill_authors" align="center"><span style="font-weight:bold;">Introduced by Assembly Member Muratsuchi<br/></span></td></tr><tr><td id="bill_intro_date" align="center" class="textcenter"><br/>August 04, 2026</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr/><br clear="all"/><div id="title"><span style="font-weight:normal;font-size:0.9em;"> Relative to Student Cancer Education, Detection, and Prevention Week. </span></div><br clear="all"/><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><h2>LEGISLATIVE COUNSEL'S DIGEST</h2></div><br/><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0"><span id="subject">HR 131, as introduced, Muratsuchi.
                          </span></div><div><span id="digesttext"/></div><span class="hidden"><h2>Digest Key</h2></span></div><hr/><span class="hidden"><h2>Bill Text</h2></span><span class="Resolution" title="source: /caml:MeasureDoc/caml:Resolution&#xA;    "><span class="Whereas" id="id_2C66BD06-A7D3-469E-9D00-F4919EC24C05"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Cancer remains one of the leading causes of death among people of all ages, and early education and awareness are critical tools in the fight against this disease; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_7AD7A5FD-4736-4D45-91A1-4B0E3ABB2A0A"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Young people, including students, benefit immensely from increased awareness about cancer risk factors, prevention strategies, early detection methods, and available resources for support and treatment; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_81124D35-0F02-4833-BA93-6B1EF93D0E0F"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Education can empower students to make healthier lifestyle choices, such as avoiding tobacco, practicing sun safety, eating a balanced diet, and being physically active, that may significantly reduce their risk of developing cancer; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_A32E0E4C-A44C-4809-9D03-4B53CB4BB034"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Many forms of cancer, when detected early, have higher rates of successful treatment and survival, highlighting the importance of informing students about early warning signs and the value of routine medical care; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_6C69976D-9017-4184-915C-294D86E28B0C"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Student Cancer Education, Detection, and Prevention Week will serve as an opportunity for schools, colleges, community organizations, health professionals, and families to come together to promote cancer education, encourage preventative behaviors, and support those affected by the disease; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_E8FA1C92-97CE-4BA6-823F-C4EFC124DAAC"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Engaging students in age-appropriate discussions about cancer not only fosters awareness but also helps reduce stigma and build compassion, resilience, and community action; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_AF88DDD1-B274-415E-927F-E58A8D6D7A29"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, It is in the interest of the State of California to support the health and well-being of its youth by fostering a culture of proactive health education; now, therefore, be it</div></span><span class="Resolved" id="id_670D37E7-97C3-4B5E-B8CC-9C5369E0E36F"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">Resolved by the Assembly of the State of California, That the Assembly proclaims October 11, 2026, through October 17, 2026, inclusive, as Student Cancer Education, Detection, and Prevention Week; and be it further</div></span><span class="Resolved" id="id_3E51B72D-09F5-4D98-9A3D-075C47443A24"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">Resolved, That the Legislature encourages middle schools, high schools, colleges, public health agencies, pediatricians, and community health providers to promote comprehensive educational campaigns on topics related to cancer prevention and detection, including risk factors, warning signs, evidence-based prevention strategies, and the importance of timely screenings and diagnostic evaluations; and be it further</div></span><span class="Resolved" id="id_96DFEFEF-FB7B-4CB0-8129-F9B2666D9406"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">Resolved, That the Chief Clerk of the Assembly transmit copies of this resolution to the author for appropriate distribution.</div></span></span></div></body></html>
